WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.650
A refund isn't just a row in a database.
On the other side... someone is waiting.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:15.510
In our simulated case, the payment went through.
The confirmation didn't. So the system tried again.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.400
Three valid refunds. Four payments.
Something had to change.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:30.020
The quick fix stopped the duplicate.
But two legitimate refunds disappeared with it.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.740
The error was gone.
The harm wasn't.

00:00:37.608 --> 00:00:41.782
NeatRescue asks a different question.
What else did this fix break?

00:00:43.817 --> 00:00:52.292
The agent reads the source, chooses diagnostics,
and replays the failure. A separate verifier checks the patch.

00:00:54.025 --> 00:01:01.502
In this recorded case: three valid refunds,
three payments. No duplicate. No legitimate request left out.

00:01:02.817 --> 00:01:09.735
After an interruption, the same saved test plan resumes.
A rejected patch doesn't get a new lottery.

00:01:12.317 --> 00:01:20.681
A direct prompt fixed this example too.
Our claim isn't a smarter model.
It's a controlled decision with inspectable evidence.

00:01:23.525 --> 00:01:29.450
We verified Neatlogs end to end. One workflow
and three tool calls were persisted.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:34.080
Then read back from the cloud,
with 117 tests passing.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:40.140
Entire provided real source context
to the investigating agent.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:45.820
We recorded the code graph and checkpoints
as evidence, not just a sponsor logo.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:54.270
cfo.ai produced a twelve-month plan
with pricing, costs, revenue and runway.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:02:02.000
Those projections are hypothetical. Today,
we have zero customers, zero sales, and zero cash.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:06.792
NeatRescue.
A fix should work for the next customer, too.
